<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Liquid glass, vendored.

    Not our code. This is KaranocaVe's LiquidGlassAvaloniaUI, MIT-licensed (see LICENSE.txt in this folder),
    taken from https://github.com/KaranocaVe/LiquidGlassAvaloniaUI at commit
    cde864d6efebc5b32484eb055900308ef4f65754 and copied in whole. It is a third assembly and the sources here
    are kept as upstream wrote them, so that a later sync with the repository is a diff rather than a merge
    across a port. Anything this tree changes in them is listed in VENDORED.md next to them.

    Why vendoring rather than a PackageReference: the package was never published - `dotnet package search
    LiquidGlassAvaloniaUI` answers with an unrelated `liquidglass.avalonia`, and the upstream repository ships
    demo and tests only, no nupkg. The alternative would have been to transcribe its shader pipeline into the
    front end's own assembly, and that is the one part of this nobody should hand-copy: 1000 lines of SkSL in
    six .sksl files plus the refraction arithmetic that feeds them.

    Two differences from upstream's own project file, both forced by this tree:
      - net10.0 instead of net8.0, and Avalonia 12.1.4 instead of 12.0.1, so that one Avalonia and one SkiaSharp
        are loaded in the process. A glass that reaches for a second SkiaSharp beside the one Avalonia.Skia came
        with is a glass that throws at its first frame.
      - AllowUnsafeBlocks, which the render-thread lock in LiquidGlassBackdropProvider compiles against and
        which therefore has to be set here rather than by whoever consumes this assembly.
    Everything else, including the assembly and namespace names, is upstream's - the shaders are loaded as
    avares://LiquidGlassAvaloniaUI/Assets/Shaders/... and that URI is built from the assembly name.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>LiquidGlassAvaloniaUI</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>LiquidGlassAvaloniaUI</AssemblyName>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia" Version="12.1.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Skia" Version="12.1.4" />
    <!--
      Pinned to the version Avalonia.Skia 12.1.4 already resolves, and stated so that a bump of Avalonia has to
      be looked at here as well: the refraction is written against Skia's runtime effects API, and a SkiaSharp
      that drifts away from the one Avalonia draws with fails at the first glass drawn, not at build time.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="SkiaSharp" Version="3.119.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <AvaloniaResource Include="Assets\**" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
